<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nyanja people</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nyanja are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantu-peoples">Bantu-speaking</a> people living around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-malawi">Lake Malawi</a> and the Shire valley in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malawi">Malawi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a>, closely related to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chewa">Chewa</a>. Their language, Chinyanja or Chichewa, is widely spoken across the region. In the nineteenth century many Nyanja villages came under the rule of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngoni">Ngoni</a> and Yao chiefs, and later of British and Portuguese colonial governments.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nyanja-people.jpg" alt="Nyanja people" width="320" height="321" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hans Hillewaert · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chewa_mask_-_Malawi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
